Don't do that.  Protection makes sense for Healers who have lots of
money and can check the health of their pet, and Tourists who have money
and can blind monsters, and archaeologists who have speed and a pickaxe
and touchstone to get more money.

I've ascended multiple wizards, one wishless; I don't claim my way is
the only way or best, but it's not worthless: *do* level up, you want
hit points and power points.  Do engage in hand-to-hand with survivable
monsters -- no need to waste a force bolt on a grid bug or newt,
especially once you have some more hit points.  I bash my way through
the dungeon until I have about 50 hp, then I go do the Mines.  I had a
long streak of not needing Sokoban: I'd find bags of holding lying
around, and wish for or make silver DSM for reflection.  Or just count
on being an elven wizard, with sleep resistance and magic protection;
with that you don't need reflection for a long time.

I can see the logic of trading spellcasting for metallic body armor, but
I'm reluctant to do so, preferring to use my spells and pet and
Elbereth.  It helps if I actually get some more spellbooks, from
sacrifice or shop or statues, of course.  Part of my thinking is hey,
I'm playing a *wizard*, I want to cast spells, not cower in mithril.

Note that magic resistance doesn't protect you from sleep spells
bouncing, and elves don't get sleep resistance until they're level 4.  

